1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,740
I was about to stop for the day,

2
00:00:01,740 --> 00:00:04,800
but I only needed three hundred more gold for
a bigger backpack.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:09,760
Well, I could just earn that little bit more,
right? And there goes another day on the farm.

4
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,960
I was trying to clock out, but the backpack
had other plans.

5
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:18,440
The first backpack upgrade in Stardew Valley
costs two thousand gold. If we imagine having

6
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,200
seventeen hundred, an abstract purchase
becomes a goal of earning three hundred more.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,820
The important part is not that the reward is
enormous.

8
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:29,980
It is knowing what I can get and how close I
am to getting it.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,120
Using Stardew Valley and Vampire Survivors,

10
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:36,140
let's explore the game design of keeping
rewards in sight.

11
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:39,700
Is it enough to put a big number on screen?
Not quite.

12
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:42,680
First, the player needs to feel why the reward
matters.

13
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:47,000
Before entering the mine, I packed my tools
and some food.

14
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:50,140
But after picking up only a few things, my
inventory is full.

15
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:53,120
Throwing something away feels wasteful, and
going home is a long trip.

16
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:58,820
A bigger backpack is no longer just an item.
It would solve a problem I am experiencing.

17
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:01,500
What happens when I see that backpack in the
shop now?

18
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:05,960
What used to be just a price tag has become
something I actually want.

19
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:08,700
When designing a game, showing how a reward
changes

20
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:12,840
play can be clearer than simply introducing
its name.

21
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:16,160
I can carry more. I can enter a new area.

22
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:21,420
I can tackle a difficult enemy in a different
way. The benefit needs to be concrete.

23
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:24,520
This does not mean endlessly adding artificial
inconvenience.

24
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:27,340
It means connecting rewards to things the
player already wants to do.

25
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:30,700
The screen says I have seventeen hundred gold.

26
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:34,360
Is that a lot? It depends on what I compare it
with.

27
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:40,820
Place the two-thousand-gold backpack price
beside it, and I immediately see

28
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:44,540
that I need three hundred more. The number now
helps me decide what to do next.

29
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:49,300
Stardew Valley does not keep the backpack
price permanently pinned to the screen.

30
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:53,240
You check the goal in the shop, then earn
money while playing.

31
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:57,400
The comparison graphic shown here is our own
illustration of that relationship.

32
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:00,300
It is not a progress bar from the actual game.

33
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:04,240
If a goal is hard to remember, a game could
let players pin a

34
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:07,900
desired item and compare their materials with
its requirements.

35
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:11,940
The key question is this: can players
understand what they

36
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:14,460
still need without repeatedly digging through
menus?

37
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:21,460
And finally, I bought the backpack. The
original twelve slots expand to twenty-four.

38
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:24,260
The satisfying part is not just the purchase
confirmation.

39
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:28,640
I can now carry the item I was thinking of
leaving behind.

40
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:33,100
I see a goal, work toward it, and gain
something that changes what I can do.

41
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,940
Connecting those steps makes the reward easier
to appreciate.

42
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:41,440
But what if a spectacular reward animation
plays and I cannot tell what improved?

43
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,940
It is like being congratulated without getting
to open the present.

44
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:50,940
For a new ability, provide a chance to use it
soon. For extra storage,

45
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:53,680
let players see the breathing room. Give them
an opportunity to experience the result.

46
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:58,860
Announcing that a number increased is
different from showing why that

47
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:01,680
increase matters. Reward design does not end
at the purchase button.

48
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:08,540
Now consider Vampire Survivors. Picking up
experience gems fills the bar at the top,

49
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:11,720
and leveling up lets you choose an upgrade.

50
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:15,620
When the bar is almost full, I want to collect
a little more experience.

51
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:21,300
Then I wonder what choices will appear and
whether I can strengthen my current weapon.

52
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:25,360
Two things coexist here. Progress toward the
next level is visible,

53
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:29,640
but that does not mean every future reward is
fully revealed in advance.

54
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:35,560
So keeping rewards visible does not mean
showing every outcome from the very beginning.

55
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:40,640
You can make progress understandable while
leaving room for choice and discovery.

56
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:43,780
Changes in your attacks then give those
choices a tangible result.

57
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,240
But a faster-filling bar is not automatically
better. If upgrades constantly interrupt play,

58
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:53,040
the rhythm of rewards needs to work with the
rhythm of action.

59
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:57,760
Should we put money, experience, materials,
and achievements on screen all at once?

60
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,300
The goals might be visible, but there might be
no room left to see the game.

61
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,200
Experience that changes during combat and a
purchase

62
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:08,300
goal set in a shop need to be checked at
different moments.

63
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:10,860
Make frequently needed information immediately
readable.

64
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:16,020
Goals checked less often can live in a menu or
a list of selected objectives.

65
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:20,500
Another option is letting players choose the
goal they care about now.

66
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:24,480
Instead of giving every reward equal
prominence, support their current intention.

67
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:28,660
The cluttered and simplified interfaces shown
here are hypothetical comparisons,

68
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:32,560
not the results of user testing on an actual
game.

69
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,120
Visible does not have to mean permanently
enormous.

70
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:40,080
Information should be easy to find when needed
and help players understand what to do next.

71
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:44,580
Here is another question. If I need just one
more material,

72
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:47,160
does that mean one more attempt will finish
the job?

73
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:50,180
Not necessarily, if obtaining it depends on
chance.

74
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:54,900
One item remaining and the time needed to get
it are different things.

75
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:00,700
This is a hypothetical design example, not a
claim about drop rates in a particular game.

76
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,400
If the interface says I am almost there but
the goal keeps feeling distant,

77
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,260
the expectation may turn into frustration.

78
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:10,320
Rather than presenting uncertain time as
guaranteed,

79
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:14,860
clearly explain the requirements and the
actions players can take.

80
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:18,760
If the goal is too distant, meaningful
intermediate changes may help.

81
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:23,620
But a stream of tiny notifications is not the
same as meaningful growth.

82
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,460
Let's bring it together. Keeping rewards
visible

83
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:30,780
is not simply putting a giant gift box on
screen.

84
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:35,580
It means connecting three things: how a reward
changes play,

85
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:38,360
how close the player is to it, and what they
can do next.

86
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:43,980
Stardew Valley's backpack connects an
immediate

87
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:46,860
inconvenience with a concrete purchase goal.

88
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:49,880
Vampire Survivors' experience bar connects
short-term progress with choices and growth.

89
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:53,780
Ask these questions about your own game. Why
would I want this reward?

90
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:58,900
Can I tell how much remains? Can I experience
the difference after earning it?

91
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:03,580
And there is an important condition: the play
along the way should be enjoyable too.

92
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:07,640
A reward for enduring boredom is not enough to
make a good experience.

93
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:12,760
Instead of a device that prevents players from
stopping, think of a reward as a promise

94
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:15,740
of something they want to try next. What could
you design from that perspective?
